  "corrections_to_earlier_version": [
    "Second-order 1/f flux dephasing was absent entirely; scqubits implements first order only and returns a divergent time at sweet spots (Groszkowski & Koch 2021 SS5.1 fn4). Added via Nguyen 2019 Eq. (A3). It contributes 0.21% of the sweet-spot rate budget, so earlier first-order-only results were sound - but this is now a computed bound rather than an assumption.",
    "CONCLUSION OVERTURNED: the earlier version reported no interior optimum over the (EJ, EL) plane and attributed the bound entirely to thermal occupation. That was an artefact of the missing second-order channel. With it, an interior optimum exists at every (EC, EL) tested, carrying 36-53%% of the total rate 1/T2 at the grid turning point. The optimum is nevertheless thermally unfavorable at 15 mK (passive initialization fails), so without active reset the practical guidance is unchanged.",
    "The extended scan now uses a Richardson-extrapolated second derivative. At the extreme corner of the extended scan (EJ=40, EL=0.2) the plain central difference at h=1e-4 is still ~4% from converged; at the interior optima themselves it is within 0.1%. The extrapolation is retained because the second-order rate is directly proportional to this derivative and the scan traverses the deep-well regime.",
    "The 10x falloff detuning is now located by Brent root-finding on the model rather than by interpolating a log grid; value 0.000996 flux quanta. The originally reported 0.0020 was the coarse grid spacing, not a measurement.",
    "Provenance previously recorded only Q_cap/A_flux/A_cc. The scqubits NOISE_PARAMS defaults (T, x_qp, Delta, t_exp, omega_low, omega_high) also determine these results and are now captured in full.",
    "Basis convergence was previously verified only at flux=0.5; it is now verified across the swept flux range down to flux=0.30.",
    "The previously quoted design optimum (T2 = 5578 us at EJ=15, EL=0.2) sits on two grid edges at f01 = 75 MHz with n_th ~ 3.7; passive initialization fails there and it is below every Nguyen 2019 Table I device. Not a design point."
  ],
  "known_artifacts": [
    "Tphi_flux (FIRST order) at flux=0.5 exceeds 1e14 us: the first-order 1/f formula with a vanishing derivative. It is not a physical dephasing time and must never be plotted or quoted. The physical value at the sweet spot is Tphi_flux2_us.",
    "Quasiparticle tunnelling is negligible ONLY at flux=0.5, where f01 is minimal. Off the sweet spot it limits T1 at most flux points. This rests on scqubits' default x_qp = 3e-6; Nguyen 2019 infer x_qp < 1e-8, at which density the channel is negligible everywhere. The finding is conditional - see the sensitivity block.",
    "T2 correlates with f01 at -0.9878 over the design plane, so raw T2 maxima reflect the frequency scaling of dielectric loss rather than design structure.",
    "Q_cap=1e6 is a FLAT override of scqubits' frequency-dependent default Q_cap(w) = 1e6*(2pi*6GHz/w)^0.7 (Smith 2020). At the sweet-spot frequency the flat value shortens T1 by 7.12x. Deliberate and conservative, and better supported than the spread suggests: Nguyen 2019 Sec. III A measure the exponent as 0.15, not 0.7, so the flat model is 1.52x from their fit while the scqubits default is 4.7x from it. Still a modelling choice, not a measurement."
  ],
